#### Article 37

Special protection as referred to in Article 25 paragraph (2) letter e, is given specifically to children, namely: children in emergency situations;

children in conflict with the law;

children from minority and isolated groups;

children exploited economically and/or sexually;

child victims of trafficking;

children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse (drugs);

child victims of abduction;

child victims of violence both physical and mental; and children with disabilities

#### Article 38

Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 37 letter a, consist of: children who become refugees;

children victims of riots;

children victims of natural disasters; and children in armed conflict situations.

Special protection for children in emergency situations as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

#### Article 39

The regional government and the community provide special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 34 letter b, which includes children in conflict with the law and child victims of criminal acts.

Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through: treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with dignity and respect for children's rights;

provision of special companion officers for children;

provision of special facilities and infrastructure;

imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the child;

continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law;

provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and protection from identity disclosure through mass media and to avoid labeling.

3. Special protection for children who become victims of criminal acts as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through: child rehabilitation efforts;

protection efforts from identity disclosure through mass media and to avoid labeling;

provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses;

provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

#### Article 40

Special protection for children from minority and isolated groups as referred to in Article 37 letter c, is carried out through the provision of facilities and infrastructure to be able to enjoy their own culture, acknowledge and practice their own religion, and use their own language.

#### Article 41

The Regional Government provides special protection for children exploited economically and/or sexually as referred to in Article 37 letter d and child victims of violence both physical and mental as referred to in Article 37 letter h.

Special protection for exploited children and child victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through: socialization of laws and regulations related to the protection of children exploited economically and/or sexually;

monitoring, reporting, and imposition of sanctions; and involvement of various organizations, Regional Government, companies, labor unions, social institutions and the community.

#### Article 42

Special protection for children involved in and victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse (drugs) as referred to in Article 37 letter f, is carried out through supervision, prevention, treatment, and rehabilitation efforts by the Regional Government and the community.

#### Article 43

The Regional Government provides special protection for child victims of trafficking as referred to in Article 37 letter e and child victims of abduction as referred to in Article 37 letter g which is carried out through efforts of: supervision;

protection;

prevention;

treatment; and rehabilitation.

#### Article 44

The Regional Government provides special protection for children with disabilities as referred to in Article 37 letter i, through efforts of: treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with dignity, and respect for children's rights;

fulfillment of special needs; and obtaining equal treatment with other children to achieve social integration as optimally as possible.

# CHAPTER VI

## OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

## Part One

General

#### Article 45

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection for women and children from acts of violence and discrimination are the joint responsibility of: the Regional Government;

the Lembang Government;

the Community; and Parents and Family.

## Part Two

Regional Government

#### Article 46

The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 45 letter a, include: establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;

conducting cooperation with other parties in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;

allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence according to regional financial capacity;

fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

providing recovery services and social reintegration;

encouraging and increasing community participation; and providing human resources in the implementation of protection of women and children victims of acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination.

In the event that a child becomes neglected as referred to in Article 22, the Regional Government is responsible and obligated to take over and provide services to neglected children as referred to in Article 36 paragraph (2) letter a.

Community institutions, and/or individuals may submit applications to the court to become guardians and/or foster parents for neglected children.

#### Article 47

In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in Article 46 paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and activities for the protection of women and children in the regional action plan as a basis for regional apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination.

The regional action plan as referred to in paragraph (1), is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).

Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

## Part Three

Lembang Government

#### Article 48

The obligations and responsibilities of the Lembang Government as referred to in Article 45 letter b, include: implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination established by the Regional Government;

conducting cooperation with other parties in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination;

allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination according to lembang financial capacity;

fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence, exploitation, trafficking and discrimination; and encouraging and increasing community participation.

## Part Four

Community
